# polarity-circuits

**Does a small language model *implement* the monotonicity calculus — or does it
merely approximate monotonicity behaviour?**

Working title: *Polarity Circuits: Is Natural-Logic Monotonicity Implemented in
Small Language Models?*

> **Status: v0.1.0 — work in progress, pre-results.**
> The engine and the benchmark generator work and are tested; the
> interpretability experiments are not written. Nothing here is a finding yet.

Roadmap:

- [x] natural-logic engine (polarity assignment + monotonicity entailment)
- [x] controlled benchmark generator with exact gold derivations
- [x] golden tests; CI green
- [ ] behavioural scoring on small LMs
- [ ] external validity on FLog / MED
- [ ] activation patching
- [ ] causal abstraction (DAS)
- [ ] KAN-vs-linear probe ablation
- [ ] paper draft -> arXiv -> workshop submission

<!-- If a checkbox is true, the corresponding code path must run. Anything else
     gets moved to a branch. -->

## The question

Natural logic (MacCartney & Manning, 2009) specifies an algorithm for
monotonicity inference: assign a polarity to every position, then allow
generalisation only in upward-entailing contexts and specialisation only in
downward-entailing ones. Models can get the *labels* right without running that
algorithm — surface cues, lexical overlap, and heuristics are enough for much of
FLog and MED.

This repo builds the instrument to tell the difference:

1. a small, dependency-free **natural-logic engine** that computes the
   ground-truth polarity tree;
2. a **controlled benchmark** generated from that engine, so the correct
   symbolic computation is known by construction;
3. **causal abstraction** experiments that ask whether the model's internal
   states behave like the symbolic variable under interchange interventions;
4. a contained **KAN-vs-linear probe** ablation on the same variable.

## Layout

```
src/polarity_circuits/
  natlog/        # engine: NO torch, NO numpy. Importable on its own.
  data/          # controlled generator + (planned) FLog / MED / FraCaS loaders
  interp/        # (planned) patching, DAS, probes -- torch lives here only
  cli.py
tests/           # golden tests; the engine's contract
configs/         # yaml, one file per experiment family
docs/            # research notes (gap memo), decisions (ADRs), data card
paper/           # ACL-style LaTeX, versioned with the code
results/         # committed numbers + figures so nobody has to rerun
```

Three structural rules, on purpose:

- **`natlog/` must never import torch.** The engine is the reusable artifact;
  keeping it light is what makes it installable and citable.
- **`tests/` is the contract.** Every bug becomes a golden case.
- **`results/` is committed.** A reader should be able to check the table
  without a GPU.

## Quickstart

```bash
uv sync --extra dev          # or: pip install -e ".[dev]"
make test
make data                    # writes data/controlled/controlled.jsonl
```

Sanity check the engine:

```python
from polarity_circuits.natlog import Node, entails, pretty, assign_polarity

p = Node.quant("every", Node.pred("animal"), Node.pred("brown"))
h = Node.quant("every", Node.pred("dog"), Node.pred("brown"))
print(pretty(assign_polarity(p)))   # every(animal↓, brown↑)
print(entails(p, h))                # True: DE restrictor, specialise
```

